@startuml
title ThingsBoard release-3.6 - service startup and shutdown

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontColor #111827
skinparam ArrowColor #111827
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #001F33
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #083B66
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #FFFFFF
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #9A3412
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #FFF7ED
skinparam NoteBorderColor #166534
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #F0FDF4
skinparam NoteFontColor #111827
skinparam DividerBorderColor #111827
skinparam DividerBackgroundColor #E5E7EB

box "One-shot database process" #FFF7ED
participant "ThingsboardInstallApplication" as InstallMain
participant "Install Spring context\n(profile=install)" as InstallCtx
participant "ThingsboardInstallService" as Installer
database "PostgreSQL / Cassandra" as InstallDb
end box

== Install or upgrade: separate process ==

InstallMain -> InstallMain: main(String[])
InstallMain -> InstallCtx: setAdditionalProfiles("install")\nrun(updateArguments(args))
InstallCtx --> InstallMain: ConfigurableApplicationContext
InstallMain -> Installer: performInstall()
alt install.upgrade = false
  Installer -> InstallDb: create entity schema
  Installer -> InstallDb: create views/functions/device_info view
  Installer -> InstallDb: create TS schema/keyspace/latest schema
  Installer -> InstallDb: load system data and optional demo
else install.upgrade = true
  Installer -> Installer: cacheCleanupService.clearCache(fromVersion)
  alt fromVersion = cassandra-latest-to-postgres
    Installer -> InstallDb: latestMigrateService.migrate()
  else fromVersion = 3.6.2-images
    Installer -> InstallDb: installScripts.updateImages()
  else regular version
    Installer -> InstallDb: fall-through upgradeDatabase(fromVersion...)
    Installer -> InstallDb: post-switch views + data/system resources
  end
end
Installer -> InstallCtx: SpringApplication.exit(context) [finally]
InstallCtx --> InstallMain: context closed

note over InstallMain,InstallDb
This context never becomes the runtime context.
Deployment orchestration must start runtime services later.
end note

newpage Runtime startup and shutdown

box "Java runtime process" #EFF6FF
participant "ThingsboardServerApplication" as Main
participant "Spring ApplicationContext" as Spring
participant "ServiceInfo + Discovery" as Discovery
participant "HashPartitionService" as Partition
participant "TbCoreStartupService" as CoreStartup
participant "TbClusterService" as Cluster
participant "DefaultActorService" as Actors
participant "Core / Rule consumers" as Consumers
participant "DefaultTransportService" as Transport
participant "MQTT / CoAP servers" as Protocols
participant "Same-order listener" as Regular
participant "Async worker/resource" as Workers
participant "Selected singleton" as Destroyed
end box

queue "Kafka / queue backend" as Broker
database "Application database" as Db

== Context refresh: dependency graph, not a global total order ==

Main -> Main: main(String[])
Main -> Main: updateArguments(args)
Main -> Spring: SpringApplication.run(...)
activate Spring

note over Spring,Protocols
Each selected singleton has its own partial order:
construct -> inject dependencies -> local @PostConstruct.
The container invokes creations one at a time by its dependency graph,
but unrelated beans have no mutual order contract.

Independent bean-local examples (list order is not execution order):
* ServiceInfo: id + ServiceType list
* Partition: queue topics + partition sizes
* Actor service: dispatchers + root actors
* Rule consumer: DB findAllQueues + managers/executors
* Transport: producer/templates + notification subscription
* MQTT/CoAP: bind protocol ports
end note

Spring -> Spring: required singleton pre-instantiation completes
note over Spring,Protocols
Protocol ports may be open before ApplicationReadyEvent.
end note

Spring -> Discovery: setTransports()\nContextRefreshedEvent
Discovery -> Spring: enumerate TbTransportService beans

== ApplicationReadyEvent ordered listeners ==

Spring -> Partition: partitionsInit() [order 1]
Spring -> Discovery: publish current service [order 2]
Discovery -> Partition: recalculatePartitions(current, others)
Partition -> Spring: publish PartitionChangeEvent(s)
Spring -> Consumers: onTbApplicationEvent(partitions)
Consumers -> Consumers: update/queue assignments
Spring -> Actors: onTbApplicationEvent(partitions)
Actors -> Actors: enqueue PartitionChangeMsg

Spring -> CoreStartup: onApplicationEvent(Ready) [order 8]
CoreStartup -> Partition: getMyPartitions(QueueKey(TB_CORE))
Partition --> CoreStartup: local core partition ids
CoreStartup -> Cluster: broadcastToCore(CoreStartupMsg)
Cluster -> Broker: publish core notification

Spring -> Actors: onApplicationEvent(Ready) [order 9]
Actors -> Actors: tellWithHighPriority(AppInitMsg)
note right of Actors
Listener returns after enqueue.
Actor-tree initialization continues asynchronously.
end note

loop order 10 listeners; one callback at a time, peer order unspecified
  Spring -> Regular: invoke next listener
  alt callback submits asynchronous work
    Regular --> Workers: submit poll loop / scheduled work
  else callback performs synchronous setup
    Regular -> Regular: subscribe or initialize template
  end
  Regular --> Spring: callback returns
end

note over Regular,Workers
Core/rule consumers, transport API and update checks are examples.
Their callbacks are serial; submitted workers may overlap later callbacks.
end note

Spring -> Transport: start() [transport order]
Transport -> Transport: launch notification poll loop
Workers -> Broker: asynchronous poll/work may already be running
deactivate Spring

... runtime: poll, callback, commit, scheduled work ...

Consumers -> Broker: poll(pack)
Consumers -> Actors: tell(message)
Actors -> Db: business transaction
Db --> Actors: commit / rollback
Actors --> Consumers: callback
Consumers -> Broker: commit offset

== Context close: dependency-driven destruction ==

Main -> Spring: shutdown hook / close()
activate Spring
note over Spring
No project ContextClosedEvent listener or SmartLifecycle phase.
Peer beans without dependency edges have no documented total order.
end note

loop eligible singletons; one destroy callback at a time, peer order unspecified
  Spring -> Destroyed: invoke next @PreDestroy / destroyMethod
  alt selected bean = ZkDiscoveryService
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: stopped=true; unpublishCurrentServer()
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: client.delete().forPath(nodePath)
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: close cache; close client; shutdownNow ZK executor
    note right of Destroyed
    Ephemeral-session removal is fallback when explicit delete fails
    or the session is already lost.
    Peer ZK watchers observe node removal later and trigger recalculation;
    this is not a direct destroy-to-partition call.
    end note
  else selected bean = Rule Engine consumer
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: stop managers; await each task up to 30s
    Destroyed --> Workers: shutdownNow context executors
  else selected bean = Actor service
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: shutdown dispatchers; await each up to 3s
    Destroyed --> Workers: shutdownNow scheduler; clear actor map
  else selected bean = transport/protocol
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: close/unsubscribe; bean-local optional wait
    Destroyed --> Workers: shutdownNow or Netty graceful shutdown
  else another singleton
    Destroyed -> Destroyed: run its bean-local destroy callback
  end
  Destroyed --> Spring: callback returns
end

Spring --> Main: context closed
deactivate Spring

note over Consumers,Db
shutdownNow is not completion. An effect committed before an
offset commit may be replayed; an open JDBC call may outlive interruption.
end note

newpage Node.js JS Executor process

box "Independent Node.js process" #F0FDF4
participant "server.ts" as NodeMain
participant "Queue template" as NodeQueue
participant "JsInvokeMessageProcessor" as JsProcessor
participant "HttpServer" as Health
end box

queue "JS request/response topics" as JsBroker

== JS Executor startup ==

NodeMain -> NodeMain: async IIFE
NodeMain -> NodeQueue: createQueue(queue_type)
NodeMain -> NodeQueue: await init()
alt queue_type = kafka
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: connect admin; list/create request topic
  NodeQueue -> NodeQueue: create consumer object; create producer object
  NodeQueue -> JsProcessor: construct JsInvokeMessageProcessor
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: connect consumer; connect producer
  NodeQueue -> NodeQueue: start response send loop
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: subscribe request topic
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: consumer.run(eachMessage)
else pubsub / aws-sqs / rabbitmq / service-bus
  NodeQueue -> NodeQueue: backend-specific init()
end
NodeQueue --> NodeMain: queue template started
NodeMain -> Health: new HttpServer(httpPort)
Health -> Health: listen + /livenessProbe

JsBroker -> NodeQueue: JS request
NodeQueue -> JsProcessor: onJsInvokeMessage(msg)
JsProcessor -> JsProcessor: compile/invoke/release
JsProcessor -> NodeQueue: send(response)
NodeQueue -> JsBroker: batched response

== JS Executor signal shutdown ==

NodeMain -> NodeMain: SIGTERM/SIGINT/uncaughtException
NodeMain -> Health: await stop()
Health -> Health: end sockets + server.close()
NodeMain -> NodeQueue: await destroy()
alt queue_type = kafka
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: disconnect admin
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: disconnect consumer
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: flush pending response batch
  NodeQueue -> JsBroker: disconnect producer
  NodeQueue -> NodeMain: process.exit(0) [KafkaTemplate only]
else pubsub / aws-sqs / rabbitmq / service-bus
  NodeQueue -> NodeQueue: backend-specific destroy()
end

note over NodeMain,JsBroker
Java RemoteJsInvokeService has a separate Spring lifecycle.
There is no cross-process ready or shutdown barrier.
Kafka admin/consumer/producer order must not be generalized to other queues.
end note

@enduml
